const o=[{id:"M01",title:"Ryan & Deci · 2000 · Self-determination theory",url:"https://selfdeterminationtheory.org/SDT/documents/2000_RyanDeci_SDT.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Motivation can reflect personally endorsed values without enjoyment. The framework proposes autonomy, competence, and relatedness as basic needs.",limitation:"Theory and narrative review, not a trial of ODA prompts. Autonomy is not independence; rewards and obligations do not automatically make a goal unowned."},{id:"M02",title:"Ntoumanis et al. · 2021 · Need-supportive health interventions",url:"https://selfdeterminationtheory.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/2020_NtoumanisEtAl_MetaAnalysisHealth.pdf",type:"research",finding:"73 studies showed modest average benefits for autonomous motivation and later health behavior. Immediate physical-health improvement was not statistically significant.",limitation:"Multicomponent health programs with substantial heterogeneity and design limits. A values prompt alone does not reproduce the program or establish effects for all goals."},{id:"M03",title:"Duckworth et al. · 2013 · Academic MCII protocol",url:"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/256294319_From_Fantasy_to_Action_Mental_Contrasting_With_Implementation_Intentions_MCII_Improves_Academic_Performance_in_Children",type:"research",finding:"A trial with 77 fifth graders used three one-hour small-group sessions and repeated outcome-obstacle-response planning. Some academic outcomes improved.",limitation:"One school, later nonsignificant between-group differences, and child-specific support/dose. No established effect for a brief adult phone exercise."},{id:"M04",title:"Cerasoli, Nicklin & Ford · 2014 · Motivation and incentives",url:"https://selfdeterminationtheory.org/wp-content/uploads/2017/06/2014_Cerasoli_Intrinsic.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Across 183 samples, intrinsic motivation and incentives both related to performance, with different quality and quantity patterns.",limitation:"Associational, heterogeneous findings do not establish universal reward prescriptions or show that socially rewarded goals are inauthentic."},{id:"M05",title:"Lally et al. · 2010 · Modeling habit formation",url:"https://www.flexyourbrain.com/wp-content/uploads/2015/10/UL-STUDY-66-Days-IJSP_998-1009.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Among 39 well-fitting curves, median modeled time to 95 percent of the automaticity plateau was 66 days. One missed opportunity did not materially disrupt the modeled process.",limitation:"Observed for 84 days; 254 days was extrapolated. Not observed completion dates, a universal timetable, or evidence about long interruptions."},{id:"M07",title:"Wood & Rünger · 2016 · Psychology of habit",url:"https://dornsife.usc.edu/wendy-wood/wp-content/uploads/sites/183/2023/10/wood.runger.2016.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Recurring contexts and repeated responses can support habit learning; deliberate goals and habitual responses can coexist.",limitation:"Narrative review, not a trial of ODA's minimum or anchor recipe. Cues do not guarantee action; small behaviors need not inevitably expand."},{id:"M08",title:"Gollwitzer & Sheeran · 2006 · If-then planning",url:"https://www.socmot.uni-konstanz.de/sites/default/files/06_Gollwitzer_Sheeran_Implementation_Intentions_And_Goal.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Specific if-then plans improved goal attainment on average across 94 independent tests. The historical standardized effect was d = 0.65.",limitation:"Varied older evidence. Not a 65 percent improvement, guaranteed execution, or benchmark for an app reminder."},{id:"M10",title:"Wang, Wang & Gai · 2021 · Mental contrasting and planning",url:"https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/psychology/articles/10.3389/fpsyg.2021.565202/full",type:"research",finding:"21 articles and 24 independent effects showed small-to-medium average goal-attainment benefits from outcome-obstacle-response planning.",limitation:"Heterogeneity and publication-bias signals limit the magnitude. No guaranteed wishes, outside-event causality, or validation of every WOOP format."},{id:"M13",title:"Harkin et al. · 2016 · Progress monitoring",url:"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/26479070/",type:"research",finding:"Monitoring interventions improved goal attainment on average across 138 randomized studies.",limitation:"Varied goals and methods. Moderator associations do not isolate recording or sharing causally; no optimal weekly cadence or required public disclosure."},{id:"M15",title:"Fogg · Tiny Habits design instructions",url:"https://tinyhabits.com/design/",type:"technique",finding:"The practitioner recipe pairs an existing routine with a tiny behavior and recommends revising unsuccessful pairings.",limitation:"No controlled estimate on this page validates the complete recipe, celebration necessity, optimal sub-minute dose, or inevitable growth."},{id:"M20",title:"NIMH · Caring for your mental health",url:"https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/caring-for-your-mental-health",type:"guidance",finding:"Persistent severe or distressing symptoms, loss of interest, or inability to complete usual activities warrant professional help. Urgent danger needs immediate assistance.",limitation:"Public guidance, not an ODA efficacy trial or diagnosis from low motivation. No need to wait two weeks, a streak, or course completion when concerned."},{id:"motivation-sdt",title:"Ryan & Deci · 2000 · Self-determination theory",url:"https://selfdeterminationtheory.org/SDT/documents/2000_RyanDeci_SDT.pdf",type:"research",finding:"Motivation can reflect personally endorsed values without enjoyment. The framework proposes autonomy, competence, and relatedness as basic needs.",limitation:"Theory and narrative review, not a trial of ODA prompts. Autonomy is not independence; rewards and obligations do not automatically make a goal unowned."},{id:"motivation-sdt-meta",title:"Ntoumanis et al. · 2021 · Need-supportive health interventions",url:"https://selfdeterminationtheory.org/wp-content/uploads/2020/05/2020_NtoumanisEtAl_MetaAnalysisHealth.pdf",type:"research",finding:"73 studies showed modest average benefits for autonomous motivation and later health behavior. Immediate physical-health improvement was not statistically significant.",limitation:"Multicomponent health programs with substantial heterogeneity and design limits. A values prompt alone does not reproduce the program or establish effects for all goals."},{id:"M16",title:"Fogg · Tiny Habits positive gesture instructions",url:"https://tinyhabits.com/rewire/",type:"technique",finding:"Fogg recommends an immediate, self-chosen positive gesture after a tiny behavior.",limitation:"Practitioner page without a controlled estimate. Celebration necessity, faster neural wiring, and checkmark equivalence were not independently established."}],i=[{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"motivation-1",courseId:"motivation",locale:"en",title:"Hate the task? Check the goal.",goal:"Choose whether a goal deserves its cost, then sample one behavior that serves it.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Does the goal on your list feel costly? Check what its effort would change in your life.",reason:{id:"motivation-1-reason",label:"What life change is worth this goal's weekly cost?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Build options for my family","Make time for curiosity","Keep a responsibility I endorse"]},why:{id:"motivation-1-why",label:"What life change is worth this goal's weekly cost?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["A task can reflect your values even when you dislike doing it. Studying for stable income, caring for family, or earning a qualification can be personally chosen. Enjoyment and ownership are different questions. Other people's approval does not automatically make a goal borrowed.","Ask what ordinary life change the goal serves and what time or energy it costs this week. Decide to keep, reshape, defer, or drop it. If you keep it, sample one useful behavior at a feasible opening. This is a choice to test, not a diagnostic test of your true motives."],example:{title:"Leila's study sample",text:"At 7 p.m., Leila weighs her qualification against family time. She dislikes the coursework but values the job options it could create. She keeps the goal and answers one practice question at the kitchen table. Her answer is incomplete. She records the attempt and chooses another opening, without pretending she enjoyed studying."}},move:{invitation:"Choose the goal, then name its actual behavior before using an example.",instructions:["Examples are editable; choose care, work, relationships, or your own behavior.","Save an available date, start signal, and place for that unit."],prompts:[{id:"motivation-1-0",label:"What are you choosing to give up for this goal?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0}],suggestions:{action:["Example: answer my qualification's first practice question","Confirm tomorrow's pickup time with Maya","Draft one sentence of my client update","Ask Sam our agreed check-in question"],minimum:["Answer that practice question","Send Maya the agreed pickup time","Write the first useful update sentence","Ask Sam how today's appointment went"],cue:["After I clear my dinner plate","When Maya's text about pickup arrives","After my 2 p.m. work break","When our agreed evening check-in starts"],place:["At my kitchen table","In our existing text thread","At my work computer","In our existing call"],minutes:[2,1,5],fallback:["Answer a shorter question from these notes","Call using our agreed contact method","Dictate that sentence in my accessible notes","Offer our agreed text check-in instead"],observation:["What I answered and what time it cost","Whether I sent the time; reply remains separate","What useful update sentence exists","Question asked, including no reply"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}],bundles:[{action:"Example: answer my qualification's first practice question",minimum:"Answer that practice question",cue:"After I clear my dinner plate",place:"At my kitchen table",fallback:"Answer a shorter question from these notes",observation:"What I answered and what time it cost",minutes:2},{action:"Confirm tomorrow's pickup time with Maya",minimum:"Send Maya the agreed pickup time",cue:"When Maya's text about pickup arrives",place:"In our existing text thread",fallback:"Call using our agreed contact method",observation:"Whether I sent the time; reply remains separate",minutes:1},{action:"Draft one sentence of my client update",minimum:"Write the first useful update sentence",cue:"After my 2 p.m. work break",place:"At my work computer",fallback:"Dictate that sentence in my accessible notes",observation:"What useful update sentence exists",minutes:5},{action:"Ask Sam our agreed check-in question",minimum:"Ask Sam how today's appointment went",cue:"When our agreed evening check-in starts",place:"In our existing call",fallback:"Offer our agreed text check-in instead",observation:"Question asked, including no reply",minutes:2}]},suggested:{action:"Example: answer my qualification's first practice question",minimum:"Answer that practice question",minutes:2,cueHint:"After I clear my dinner plate",placeHint:"At my kitchen table",obstacleHint:"Example: today's question competes with family time",fallbackHint:"Answer a shorter question from these notes",observationHint:"What I answered and what time it cost"},stopRule:"Do not push through illness, danger, or persistent difficulty functioning. Support is available now.",accessibleAlternative:"Use audio, dictation, assistive tools, agreed help, or an explicit pause."},result:{question:"Did you try the behavior you chose?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what did it cost?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"motivation-1-decision",scenario:"Leila dislikes studying for a qualification. She wants more stable job options for her family, and her colleagues also value the credential.",question:"What best tests whether to keep it?",options:[{id:"motivation-1-endorse",text:"Check whether she endorses the benefit and time cost, then answer one practice question.",feedback:"This separates dislike from ownership and tests a feasible behavior. Recognition can coexist with a chosen reason. She still gets to reshape or defer the goal if its cost does not fit."},{id:"motivation-1-interest",text:"Try a more enjoyable goal for a week before deciding whether this qualification still belongs to her.",feedback:"Exploring another interest can be useful, but enjoyment alone cannot decide whether this qualification is owned. First examine its chosen benefit and cost; a valued task may remain unpleasant."},{id:"motivation-1-advice",text:"Ask credentialed colleagues which qualification they would choose, then adopt the one they recommend.",feedback:"Colleagues can clarify options and requirements. Their recommendation cannot decide Leila's trade-off for her. She needs her own reason and feasible time cost before adopting their goal."}],bestOptionId:"motivation-1-endorse"}},carry:{takeaway:"A chosen goal can be difficult. Judge its value and cost, then test its behavior.",followUp:{questionId:"motivation-1-return",question:"Did the sample serve your chosen reason, and is its cost still worth accepting?",observationPrompt:"What did you actually do, and what did it cost?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change would make your next attempt fit?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Ownership does not require enjoyment",paragraphs:["Ryan and Deci's self-determination theory distinguishes intrinsic enjoyment from personally endorsed reasons for doing something. An extrinsic goal can become valued: a qualification, income, care, or a shared responsibility may matter even when the behavior is dull. Autonomy means a sense of choice, not having no obligations or influences. Their 2000 paper is a theory and narrative review, not a trial of this lesson. [M01]","This lesson's keep, reshape, defer, or drop decision is an ODA reflection exercise. It does not reveal pure motives or certify that a goal is authentic. Cerasoli and colleagues' large meta-analysis found intrinsic motivation and incentives can both relate to performance, with variation across quality and quantity outcomes. It does not show rewards are universally good or bad. [M04] You can acknowledge mixed motives and still decide whether the behavior serves the life you want."]},{heading:"A values prompt is not a whole program",paragraphs:["Ntoumanis and colleagues pooled 73 self-determination-informed health intervention studies. They found modest average benefits for autonomous motivation and later health behavior. The programs included multiple supports; findings varied substantially. Immediate physical health improvement was not statistically significant. These results do not establish the same benefit for every career, study, or personal goal. [M02]","A single reason prompt cannot reproduce those programs. Here, your reason guides a practical experiment: one goal, one actual behavior, one honest record, and one next opportunity. Use the first sample to notice fit, access, and cost without demanding enthusiasm. Keep details private if you prefer. Later lessons keep this same direction while testing a meaningful minimum, a usable cue, and a response to a real obstacle. An explicit pause or request for support is a valid choice when the goal or circumstances need attention."]}],sourceIds:["M01","M04","M02"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"motivation-2",courseId:"motivation",locale:"en",title:"Opened the book again without reading?",goal:"Try a small version of your existing behavior that still produces something useful.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Opened the book without reading? Make the useful part small enough to do.",reason:{id:"motivation-2-useful-unit-cost",label:"What preparation could you stop to make room for the useful part?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Stop reopening; read one sentence","Stop rearranging; write one sentence","Keep the useful act; shorten its size"]},why:{id:"motivation-2-why",label:"What useful minimum would serve your chosen reason?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Keep the goal you chose. Shrink its behavior until it fits today, while keeping the useful part inside. Put the book within reach, then read a sentence. Open the application, then write one useful sentence. The setup is preparation; the sentence is the behavior you want to test.","Habit research describes repeated responses in recurring contexts. It does not show that every tiny start grows, or that a checkmark has the same effect as celebration. Your minimum can stay small. Try it once, record what actually happened, and later ask whether that amount still serves your reason."],example:{title:"Leila's smaller question",text:"On Tuesday after dinner, Leila has two minutes for the same qualification. She puts her notes within reach, then answers one short practice question. She stops there because family time matters too. Her answer needs correction. She records the answer as work and the notes setup as preparation, without expanding the session."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your saved behavior; shorten its useful unit before saving.",instructions:["Retain your action and usable setting; edit only the useful minimum.","Put needed materials within reach, then perform that minimum."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Example: answer my next qualification question","Write my saved application paragraph","Complete my agreed kitchen cleanup"],minimum:["Answer the first part of that question","Write one usable sentence of that paragraph","Wash the two mugs we agreed on"],cue:["After I clear my dinner plate","After my 2 p.m. work break","After our shared meal ends"],place:["At my kitchen table","At my work computer","At my accessible kitchen sink"],minutes:[2,1],fallback:["Dictate the first part from accessible notes","Dictate one usable sentence in my notes","Choose another agreed accessible cleanup task"],observation:["Actual answer versus materials only prepared","Sentence written versus document only opened","Agreed task done versus supplies only arranged"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}],bundles:[{action:"Example: answer my next qualification question",minimum:"Answer the first part of that question",cue:"After I clear my dinner plate",place:"At my kitchen table",fallback:"Dictate the first part from accessible notes",observation:"Actual answer versus materials only prepared",minutes:2},{action:"Write my saved application paragraph",minimum:"Write one usable sentence of that paragraph",cue:"After my 2 p.m. work break",place:"At my work computer",fallback:"Dictate one usable sentence in my notes",observation:"Sentence written versus document only opened",minutes:1},{action:"Complete my agreed kitchen cleanup",minimum:"Wash the two mugs we agreed on",cue:"After our shared meal ends",place:"At my accessible kitchen sink",fallback:"Choose another agreed accessible cleanup task",observation:"Agreed task done versus supplies only arranged",minutes:2}]},suggested:{action:"Example: answer my next qualification question",minimum:"Answer the first part of that question",minutes:2,cueHint:"After I clear my dinner plate",placeHint:"At my kitchen table",obstacleHint:"Example: I prepare the materials without answering",fallbackHint:"Dictate the first part from accessible notes",observationHint:"Actual answer versus materials only prepared"},stopRule:"Choose a safe unit. Stop for pain, illness, urgent needs, or an inaccessible setup.",accessibleAlternative:"Listen, dictate, use assistive tools, or choose labeled preparation and a later real opportunity."},result:{question:"Did you do the useful part of your minimum?",observationPrompt:"What was actual behavior, and what was preparation?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"motivation-2-decision",scenario:"You want to write an application. For three sessions, you opened the file but wrote nothing. You have two minutes and know the required section.",question:"Which minimum now tests useful progress?",options:[{id:"motivation-2-bigger",text:"Reserve a longer session later so a complete answer will feel worth starting.",feedback:"A longer session may help completion, but it leaves the current preparation-only pattern untested. Use a rough sentence now and decide later whether a larger slot is needed."},{id:"motivation-2-outline",text:"Use the two minutes to outline three points, then write the required section in a longer slot.",feedback:"An outline can clarify content. You already know this section, and the repeated barrier is starting its prose. A rough sentence tests that barrier now; record any outlining as preparation."},{id:"motivation-2-sentence",text:"Write one rough sentence for the required section, then stop at the available limit.",feedback:"This contains the behavior and leaves an editable piece. It does not guarantee the application's success or require a longer session. Record the sentence separately from opening the file."}],bestOptionId:"motivation-2-sentence"}},carry:{takeaway:"Make the start feasible without shrinking the useful behavior out of it.",followUp:{questionId:"motivation-2-return",question:"Did your small version produce useful behavior, or only make the setup ready?",observationPrompt:"What was actual behavior, and what was preparation?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change would make your next attempt fit?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Small is a design choice, not a guarantee",paragraphs:["Wood and Rünger review habits as learned responses connected with recurring contexts. Repetition can support greater automaticity, while deliberate goals can still matter. Their review does not establish that one sentence is an optimal dose or that every small behavior inevitably expands. This lesson uses a small unit to test access and usefulness in your actual week. [M07]","Use the unit to separate preparation from the behavior you want to practice. A question answered can be checked, a sentence read can convey information, and a sentence written can be edited. Having materials ready is useful setup, but it does not contain those behaviors. If several tiny attempts never serve the reason you chose, review the behavior or goal. You may need a different unit, more support, or a larger feasible window. Do not turn a convenient checkmark into invented progress. [M07]"]},{heading:"The practitioner recipe has limits",paragraphs:["BJ Fogg's Tiny Habits instruction pairs an existing routine with a tiny behavior and recommends revising a pairing that does not work. It is a practitioner technique. The inspected page does not independently establish that its whole recipe outperforms ordinary cue planning or that every action must take less than a minute. [M15]","Fogg also recommends an immediate, self-chosen positive gesture. The proposed claims about faster neural wiring, celebration being necessary, or a checkmark doing the same reinforcing job were not established by controlled evidence in the dossier. [M16] You may notice satisfaction if it occurs, but do not manufacture it. A truthful record says what happened. It is separate from how you felt afterward. You can stop at the useful minimum, retain it tomorrow, and adapt your tools or access method without treating a smaller amount as lesser character."]}],sourceIds:["M07","M15","M16"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"motivation-3",courseId:"motivation",locale:"en",title:"Reminder rang. You still couldn't start.",goal:"Test your existing minimum at a cue where time, materials, and access are available.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Reminder ringing while you are on the bus? Choose a cue with a usable setting.",reason:{id:"motivation-3-available-setting-cost",label:"Which available setting will you use, and what will you move to protect it?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Use the fixed break I can protect","Move one flexible chore","Keep care covered while I practice"]},why:{id:"motivation-3-why",label:"Which real opening protects time for your chosen goal?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Your start signal has three jobs: it happens, you notice it, and the behavior is possible then. After the school run cannot support desk work if you go straight to your job. Keep your useful minimum; choose a setting that permits it.","Specific if-then plans can help bridge intention and action. Repeating a behavior in a recurring context can support a habit, but no finish date fits everyone. Today, test the cue rather than trying to become automatic. Record which of the three jobs worked and which needs an adjustment."],example:{title:"Leila's usable opening",text:"Leila planned her question after the school run, but went straight to work without her notes. On Wednesday she moves the same question to after dinner at the kitchen table. The cue happens and she notices it. She answers before clearing the table. One answer is useful; the earlier unavailable slot stays in her record."}},move:{invitation:"Keep your saved unit; choose an available event and place.",instructions:["Check whether the start signal happens, gets noticed, and permits your saved behavior.","Choose the date and confirm materials or access will be there."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Example: answer my saved qualification question","Write my saved application sentence","Complete my agreed kitchen cleanup"],minimum:["Answer that same practice question","Write that same useful sentence","Wash the same two agreed mugs"],cue:["After I clear my dinner plate","When my fixed 12:30 break starts","After our shared meal ends"],place:["At my kitchen table","At my accessible break desk","At my accessible kitchen sink"],minutes:[2,1],fallback:["Use my accessible notes at tomorrow's meal","Dictate the sentence using my saved notes","Use the next agreed covered cleanup slot"],observation:["Signal happened, noticed, and behavior possible","Whether this setting allowed the same unit","Signal happened and agreed task was possible"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}],bundles:[{action:"Example: answer my saved qualification question",minimum:"Answer that same practice question",cue:"After I clear my dinner plate",place:"At my kitchen table",fallback:"Use my accessible notes at tomorrow's meal",observation:"Signal happened, noticed, and behavior possible",minutes:2},{action:"Write my saved application sentence",minimum:"Write that same useful sentence",cue:"When my fixed 12:30 break starts",place:"At my accessible break desk",fallback:"Dictate the sentence using my saved notes",observation:"Whether this setting allowed the same unit",minutes:1},{action:"Complete my agreed kitchen cleanup",minimum:"Wash the same two agreed mugs",cue:"After our shared meal ends",place:"At my accessible kitchen sink",fallback:"Use the next agreed covered cleanup slot",observation:"Signal happened and agreed task was possible",minutes:2}]},suggested:{action:"Example: answer my saved qualification question",minimum:"Answer that same practice question",minutes:2,cueHint:"After I clear my dinner plate",placeHint:"At my kitchen table",obstacleHint:"Example: my old signal occurred without my notes",fallbackHint:"Use my accessible notes at tomorrow's meal",observationHint:"Signal happened, noticed, and behavior possible"},stopRule:"Do not practice while driving or where it interferes with care or safety.",accessibleAlternative:"Use a visible or accessible reminder, audio materials, agreed assistance, or a later suitable cue."},result:{question:"Did your cue allow the minimum to happen?",observationPrompt:"Did the cue happen, get noticed, and allow the behavior?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"motivation-3-usable-setting-decision",scenario:"Leila wants to answer her saved question today. Her school-run cue failed because work starts immediately. Dinner offers five confirmed minutes with notes. Her fixed lunch break allows a phone copy. Tomorrow's train also allows study, but no departure is confirmed.",question:"Which change most directly tests the same unit today?",options:[{id:"motivation-3-dinner",text:"Use the confirmed dinner slot with her existing notes and answer the same saved question.",feedback:"This changes the unavailable opening while retaining the unit and materials. The slot is confirmed today. Record whether the signal happens, is noticed, and actually permits the answer."},{id:"motivation-3-phone-copy",text:"Use lunch to make a phone copy, then answer the question during a later available break.",feedback:"Portable notes could support future work breaks. This choice uses today’s known opening for setup and leaves answering undated. Dinner tests the saved behavior today with materials already available."},{id:"motivation-3-train",text:"Carry the notes for tomorrow’s train and answer the same question once its departure is confirmed.",feedback:"The train could provide a workable setting after confirmation. It postpones a test despite an available slot today. If dinner stops fitting, confirm the train date before committing to it."}],bestOptionId:"motivation-3-dinner"}},carry:{takeaway:"A usable cue happens, gets noticed, and allows the behavior in that setting.",followUp:{questionId:"motivation-3-return",question:"Which cue condition failed: happening, noticing, or being able to act?",observationPrompt:"Did the cue happen, get noticed, and allow the behavior?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change would make your next attempt fit?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"A cue is a condition to test",paragraphs:["Gollwitzer and Sheeran's 2006 review combined 94 independent tests of plans connecting a situation with a chosen action. Goal attainment improved on average. The historical standardized effect of 0.65 is not a 65 percent improvement, and it does not forecast the effect of your app reminder. These historical averages concern varied goals and settings rather than this exact study cue. [M08]","Wood and Rünger describe context-linked repetition as part of habit learning. A cue alone does not guarantee behavior, and every routine is not necessarily automatic. [M07] ODA's happens, noticed, possible check is a teaching aid. It helps separate a missed reminder from unavailable materials or a competing duty. If the cue did not happen, record no occasion when appropriate. If it happened but you did not act, record that fact without turning it into a verdict about your commitment."]},{heading:"Sixty-six days was a model estimate",paragraphs:["Lally's 2010 study observed chosen daily behaviors for 84 days. Among 39 participants with well-fitting automaticity curves, the median model-estimated time to 95 percent of the plateau was 66 days, with estimates from 18 to 254. The longest estimate extended beyond observation. These were not observed fastest, average, and slowest habit-completion dates. [M05]","Your qualification practice is not a copy of the study's eating, drinking, or activity behaviors. Do not use its estimate as a deadline or proof that practice should become effortless. Keep the same experiment and look at available opportunities, useful behavior, and the setup that supported it. One cue can fit weekdays while a different arrangement is needed during shifts or care. Test a deliberate change and retain the earlier record so your review compares actual conditions."]}],sourceIds:["M08","M07","M05"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"motivation-4",courseId:"motivation",locale:"en",title:"Plan for the moment it breaks",goal:"Test one feasible response to an observed obstacle while protecting real needs and responsibilities.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Study slot colliding with bedtime care? A stronger pep talk cannot cover childcare.",reason:{id:"motivation-4-accepted-change-cost",label:"Which concrete change will you accept to address the barrier your record shows?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Move the slot; keep the useful unit","Request specific support before assuming coverage","Change access rather than demand more effort"]},why:{id:"motivation-4-why",label:"What needs protecting while this goal moves forward?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Retain your goal and useful unit. Use a real attempt to identify one barrier: switching away, missing materials, competing care, or a health need. Pairing an outcome, obstacle, and response shows modest average research benefits. It cannot make missing time or help appear.","Match the response to the condition. A distracting tab may need a changed setup. Care needs covered time or agreed help. Illness may call for rest. Plan the first useful behavior your response allows, then test it at the next suitable opening. Rehearsal is preparation, not evidence the fallback worked."],example:{title:"Leila's covered time",text:"Thursday's study cue arrives during Leila's child's bedtime. Her partner agrees to cover five minutes after bedtime, so she moves the same question there. She answers at the table while care remains covered. She records the arrangement and the answer. The question needs work, and tomorrow's coverage is not assumed."}},move:{invitation:"Match one changed condition to your recorded barrier; retain your useful act.",instructions:["Coverage agreed: try your retained unit; change only the barrier your record identifies.","No agreement: choose Adapt, Support; request a specific task, person, and time.","If declined, use a genuinely available slot or pause; support remains separate from practice."],prompts:[{id:"motivation-4-support-request",label:"If you need support, what task, person, and time will your request name?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,visible:!0,help:"Example: ask Maya by text for five-minute care cover."}],suggestions:{action:["Example: write my saved application sentence","Answer my saved question during agreed cover","Read my saved passage using audio"],minimum:["Write that same useful application sentence","Answer that same practice question","Listen to that same useful passage"],cue:["After my 2 p.m. work break","When the agreed five-minute care cover starts","When my confirmed 12:30 break starts"],place:["At my work computer","At my kitchen table","At my quiet accessible break space"],minutes:[2,5],fallback:["Silence optional alerts; keep urgent contact available","If cover falls through, pause the practice","Request an accessible version if audio fails"],observation:["Whether alerts appeared and my response allowed writing","Coverage available and question answered are separate","Access available and passage heard are separate"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}],bundles:[{action:"Example: write my saved application sentence",minimum:"Write that same useful application sentence",cue:"After my 2 p.m. work break",place:"At my work computer",fallback:"Silence optional alerts; keep urgent contact available",observation:"Whether alerts appeared and my response allowed writing",minutes:2},{action:"Answer my saved question during agreed cover",minimum:"Answer that same practice question",cue:"When the agreed five-minute care cover starts",place:"At my kitchen table",fallback:"If cover falls through, pause the practice",observation:"Coverage available and question answered are separate",minutes:2},{action:"Read my saved passage using audio",minimum:"Listen to that same useful passage",cue:"When my confirmed 12:30 break starts",place:"At my quiet accessible break space",fallback:"Request an accessible version if audio fails",observation:"Access available and passage heard are separate",minutes:5}]},suggested:{action:"Example: write my saved application sentence",minimum:"Write that same useful application sentence",minutes:2,cueHint:"After my 2 p.m. work break",placeHint:"At my work computer",obstacleHint:"Example: optional phone alerts interrupted my last sentence",fallbackHint:"Silence optional alerts; keep urgent contact available",observationHint:"Whether alerts appeared and my response allowed writing"},stopRule:"Keep care and urgent needs covered. Choose support or rest for illness or unsafe conditions.",accessibleAlternative:"Request agreed assistance, change access or timing, or choose a support or rest action."},result:{question:"Did your response allow your chosen minimum?",observationPrompt:"Did the obstacle appear, and what response did you use?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"motivation-4-covered-slot-decision",scenario:"Guitar practice overlaps bedtime care. Tonight’s five-minute cover is confirmed, and the guitar is ready. A neighbor might offer a longer Saturday slot but has not agreed. The parent wants to try one short phrase tonight while protecting care.",question:"Which response best fits that next attempt?",options:[{id:"motivation-4-confirmed-cover",text:"Play the short phrase during tonight’s confirmed five-minute cover, then stop when that cover ends.",feedback:"The agreement supplies the missing condition without enlarging the act. Try the retained phrase and record practice separately from coverage. Tonight’s arrangement does not imply future help is booked."},{id:"motivation-4-saturday-request",text:"Ask the neighbor about Saturday’s longer covered slot and wait for confirmation before scheduling that practice.",feedback:"This is a safe support request for a later opportunity. It does not test tonight’s already feasible short phrase. Choose it if tonight stops fitting, and leave Saturday unconfirmed until agreed."},{id:"motivation-4-prepare-first",text:"Use tonight’s covered five minutes to check tuning and mark the phrase for another practice slot.",feedback:"Tuning and marking can be useful preparation when needed. The instrument and phrase are ready here. Preparing again leaves the actual practice untested; use the confirmed cover for the phrase."}],bestOptionId:"motivation-4-confirmed-cover"}},carry:{takeaway:"A response works only if it fits the obstacle and leaves real needs protected.",followUp:{questionId:"motivation-4-barrier-response-return",question:"Did your saved act happen, and did the changed condition address the recorded barrier?",observationPrompt:"What was practice, what was support, and which constraint remained?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change would make your next attempt fit?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Research supports planning, with dose limits",paragraphs:["Wang and colleagues' 2021 review pooled 21 articles and 24 effects of mental contrasting with implementation intentions. Its average goal-attainment effect was small to medium. Results varied across goals and showed publication-bias signals. Combining an outcome, obstacle, and response does not guarantee wishes, demonstrate that thoughts change outside events, or establish every WOOP format as equivalent. [M10]","Duckworth and colleagues tested an academic protocol with 77 fifth graders at one school. Students worked in small groups across three one-hour sessions, with repeated exercises and reminder cards. Some academic outcomes improved, but later between-group differences were nonsignificant. [M03] ODA's short adult exercise changes population, support, and duration. It is an adaptation for choosing a testable response, not the full intervention. Rehearse if useful, then distinguish preparation from the actual opportunity when the obstacle appears and the response is needed."]},{heading:"Match the barrier without blaming the person",paragraphs:["The planning procedure examines a desired outcome, an obstacle, and a chosen counteraction. It does not establish that every obstacle is an inner attitude. Care, money, disability access, shift work, illness, and another person's harmful conduct can constrain what is feasible. A practical response may be agreed support, a changed schedule, an accommodation, rest, or a boundary. The adult adaptation should name those constraints honestly. [M03, M10]","Use your actual record to choose one condition to change. If the cue happened and materials were unavailable, a bigger commitment misses the problem. If care needed you, leave that responsibility covered. If a distracting habit appeared, test a response that still allows urgent contact. An obstacle that never occurred leaves its fallback untested. Record the useful unit separately from arranging help or moving materials. Later, consolidate the setup that worked rather than collecting more rituals or demanding a stronger feeling of motivation."]}],sourceIds:["M10","M03"]}},{schemaVersion:2,revision:2,id:"motivation-5",courseId:"motivation",locale:"en",title:"A missed day needs a workable return",goal:"Review the same experiment, retain one useful condition, and schedule a feasible next attempt.",minutes:{learning:6,suggestedAction:2,review:2},direction:{hook:"Missed sessions? Did your goal change, or did your available time?",reason:{id:"motivation-5-reason",label:"Does this goal still deserve a place next week?",kind:"text",maxLength:160,optional:!0,suggestions:["Return to a goal I still choose","Change a cue that no longer fits","Protect rest and seek needed support"]},why:{id:"motivation-5-why",label:"Does this goal still deserve a place next week?",kind:"text",maxLength:300,optional:!0}},idea:{paragraphs:["Review your chosen reason, useful minimum, cue, and actual records. Progress monitoring can support goal attainment on average. It cannot turn an untried plan into behavior or make blank history mean failure. Keep preparation, actual work, and pause distinct when you decide what helped.","One missed opportunity did not materially disrupt habit formation in one study. That does not establish that long interruptions are harmless. Repeat a setup that fit, change an unavailable opening, or reconsider the goal. Date the next useful unit. No doubling up or earned permission to rest is required."],example:{title:"Leila's next opening",text:"On Sunday, Leila reviews the same qualification plan. Dinner slots worked twice; a changed shift blocked two others. She still chooses the goal, so she moves one question to Monday's available lunch break with accessible notes. She writes down the next opening. The missed sessions stay missed, and Monday's action remains a plan."}},move:{invitation:"Review your record; date the same useful unit at an available opening.",instructions:["Change only the condition your attempts identify; confirm its date and setting.","Only this occurrence and its next-day return are booked; weekly review remains your choice."],prompts:[],suggestions:{action:["Example: answer my saved qualification question","Take my saved short walk","Complete my agreed kitchen cleanup"],minimum:["Answer that same practice question","Walk the same safe five-minute route","Wash the same two agreed mugs"],cue:["When my confirmed 12:30 lunch break starts","When my fixed noon break starts","After our shared meal ends"],place:["At my accessible break desk","On my accessible workplace loop","At my accessible kitchen sink"],minutes:[2,5],fallback:["If lunch is canceled, date another feasible slot","If route is unsafe, choose seated movement","If unavailable, agree another useful cleanup task"],observation:["Whether revised opening allowed the same question","Actual movement and available time","Task done and whether the setting fit"],when:[{label:"Today",daysFromToday:0},{label:"Tomorrow",daysFromToday:1}],bundles:[{action:"Example: answer my saved qualification question",minimum:"Answer that same practice question",cue:"When my confirmed 12:30 lunch break starts",place:"At my accessible break desk",fallback:"If lunch is canceled, date another feasible slot",observation:"Whether revised opening allowed the same question",minutes:2},{action:"Take my saved short walk",minimum:"Walk the same safe five-minute route",cue:"When my fixed noon break starts",place:"On my accessible workplace loop",fallback:"If route is unsafe, choose seated movement",observation:"Actual movement and available time",minutes:5},{action:"Complete my agreed kitchen cleanup",minimum:"Wash the same two agreed mugs",cue:"After our shared meal ends",place:"At my accessible kitchen sink",fallback:"If unavailable, agree another useful cleanup task",observation:"Task done and whether the setting fit",minutes:2}]},suggested:{action:"Example: answer my saved qualification question",minimum:"Answer that same practice question",minutes:2,cueHint:"When my confirmed 12:30 lunch break starts",placeHint:"At my accessible break desk",obstacleHint:"Example: my recorded shift change removed evening study time",fallbackHint:"If lunch is canceled, date another feasible slot",observationHint:"Whether revised opening allowed the same question"},stopRule:"Persistent loss of interest or difficulty functioning deserves support now. Use urgent help for immediate danger.",accessibleAlternative:"Use accessible materials, agreed assistance, a support request, rest, or an explicit pause."},result:{question:"Did you carry out your chosen return?",observationPrompt:"What actual behavior, support, or pause did you record?"},judgment:{primary:{id:"motivation-5-recorded-barrier-decision",scenario:"Kofi’s new shift begins before dinner ends, removing his evening walk window. He still chooses walking. A safe five-minute lunch route is available daily. A safe pre-shift route requires waking early; a longer Sunday route is available. He wants to test a weekday return without reducing sleep.",question:"Which plan best fits the recorded barrier and cost?",options:[{id:"motivation-5-early-walk",text:"Move the walk before his shift and set an earlier alarm to protect that new opening.",feedback:"A pre-shift route solves the evening conflict, but the earlier alarm conflicts with his sleep boundary. The available lunch slot tests a weekday return without that additional cost."},{id:"motivation-5-sunday-walk",text:"Switch to a longer Sunday walk and reconsider whether a weekly cadence better fits his schedule.",feedback:"A weekly walk is a feasible goal revision, not a failure. Kofi currently wants a weekday test, and lunch is available. Try that condition before deciding whether to change his cadence."},{id:"motivation-5-lunch-return",text:"Date the next lunch opening, take the safe five-minute walk, and record whether the new timing fits.",feedback:"This retains walking and its useful unit while addressing the unavailable evening slot. Only the actual walk counts as practice. Tomorrow’s review can guide repeating, changing, or stopping this arrangement."}],bestOptionId:"motivation-5-lunch-return"}},carry:{takeaway:"Keep the reason and useful behavior; change the condition your record says has failed.",followUp:{questionId:"motivation-5-return",question:"Did your revised opening fit, and is the goal still worth its cost next week?",observationPrompt:"What actual behavior, support, or pause did you record?",adjustmentPrompt:"What one change would make your next attempt fit?"}},goDeeper:{sections:[{heading:"Consolidate evidence without inventing a streak",paragraphs:["Harkin and colleagues' review of 138 randomized studies found an average goal-attainment benefit from progress monitoring. Goals and methods varied. Recording and reporting moderators were associated with larger effects, but those results do not establish that public sharing or a weekly cadence is necessary. ODA's next-day return is an adaptation, not a validated dose. A weekly review is optional and is not automatically booked by this plan. [M13]","Lally's observational study found that one missed opportunity did not materially disrupt its modeled habit-formation process. That narrow result does not prove long interruptions leave a habit unchanged. [M05] Use your own attempts to choose what comes next. Keep a useful unit, change a cue or response that did not fit, and leave historical misses visible. A future plan is still planned. If the goal no longer deserves its time cost, reshape, defer, or stop it deliberately. Learning completion is separate from doing and reviewing the behavior."]},{heading:"Enjoyment is optional; support can come now",paragraphs:["Your final lesson consolidates the experiment rather than adding a new motivational ritual. Monitoring research supports comparing actual progress with a chosen goal on average, but it does not identify which technique is best for every person. Your record can show whether a cue was unavailable, whether materials were missing, or whether the goal's time cost stopped fitting. Use that observed condition to choose one next change. Do not count preparation or a promise as the behavior. [M13]","Do not make basic rest or all enjoyable activity conditional on productivity. If you later choose compatible free audio, preserve traffic and safety awareness. Persistent loss of interest, distress, exhaustion, or trouble completing usual activities may need professional assessment. NIMH guidance supports seeking help and immediate assistance for urgent danger; it does not diagnose the cause from low motivation or require waiting before asking for help. [M20] You can keep a small useful practice, request support, or pause while that concern is addressed."]}],sourceIds:["M13","M05","M20"]}}],n={id:"motivation",title:"Waiting for motivation? Give your goal a real opening.",subtitle:"Five lessons to choose a goal worth its cost, test a useful minimum, and make the setup fit your week.",description:"Choose what the effort is for, do a useful small version, and adjust the cue or support using what actually happened.",scope:"Training for everyday goals. Constant lack of drive, exhaustion or losing the ability to function may not be only a motivation problem; it is a good idea to get support, and a health professional can help you look at the cause.",outcome:"A chosen action and a dated next attempt shaped by your records.",photo:{id:"1790004245013-b347139d5591",alt:"A man running along a sunlit forest path"},lessons:[{id:"motivation-1",schemaVersion:2,title:"Hate the task? Check the goal.",minutes:6,goal:"Choose whether a goal deserves its cost, then sample one behavior that serves it.",reading:["A task can reflect your values even when you dislike doing it. Studying for stable income, caring for family, or earning a qualification can be personally chosen. Enjoyment and ownership are different questions. Other people's approval does not automatically make a goal borrowed.","Ask what ordinary life change the goal serves and what time or energy it costs this week. Decide to keep, reshape, defer, or drop it. If you keep it, sample one useful behavior at a feasible opening. This is a choice to test, not a diagnostic test of your true motives."],practice:["Decide: keep, reshape, defer, or drop.","If continuing, try one useful unit.","Try the minimum: Answer one practice question."],reflection:"What life change is worth this goal's weekly cost?",question:"Leila dislikes studying for a qualification. She wants more stable job options for her family, and her colleagues also value the credential. What best tests whether to keep it?",options:["Try a more enjoyable goal for a week before deciding whether this qualification still belongs to her.","Check whether she endorses the benefit and time cost, then answer one practice question.","Ask credentialed colleagues which qualification they would choose, then adopt the one they recommend."],correct:1,feedback:"This separates dislike from ownership and tests a feasible behavior. Recognition can coexist with a chosen reason. She still gets to reshape or defer the goal if its cost does not fit.",takeaway:"A chosen goal can be difficult. Judge its value and cost, then test its behavior.",sources:["M01","M04","M02"],deeper:[{heading:"Ownership does not require enjoyment",paragraphs:["Ryan and Deci's self-determination theory distinguishes intrinsic enjoyment from personally endorsed reasons for doing something. An extrinsic goal can become valued: a qualification, income, care, or a shared responsibility may matter even when the behavior is dull. Autonomy means a sense of choice, not having no obligations or influences. Their 2000 paper is a theory and narrative review, not a trial of this lesson. [M01]","This lesson's keep, reshape, defer, or drop decision is an ODA reflection exercise. It does not reveal pure motives or certify that a goal is authentic. Cerasoli and colleagues' large meta-analysis found intrinsic motivation and incentives can both relate to performance, with variation across quality and quantity outcomes. It does not show rewards are universally good or bad. [M04] You can acknowledge mixed motives and still decide whether the behavior serves the life you want."]},{heading:"A values prompt is not a whole program",paragraphs:["Ntoumanis and colleagues pooled 73 self-determination-informed health intervention studies. They found modest average benefits for autonomous motivation and later health behavior. The programs included multiple supports; findings varied substantially. Immediate physical health improvement was not statistically significant. These results do not establish the same benefit for every career, study, or personal goal. [M02]","A single reason prompt cannot reproduce those programs. Here, your reason guides a practical experiment: one goal, one actual behavior, one honest record, and one next opportunity. Use the first sample to notice fit, access, and cost without demanding enthusiasm. Keep details private if you prefer. Later lessons keep this same direction while testing a meaningful minimum, a usable cue, and a response to a real obstacle. An explicit pause or request for support is a valid choice when the goal or circumstances need attention."]}],example:{title:"Leila's study sample",text:"At 7 p.m., Leila weighs her qualification against family time. She dislikes the coursework but values the job options it could create. She keeps the goal and answers one practice question at the kitchen table. Her answer is incomplete. She records the attempt and chooses another opening, without pretending she enjoyed studying."},photo:{id:"1517971129774-8a2b38fa128e",alt:"A woman writing in a notebook by a window"}},{id:"motivation-2",schemaVersion:2,title:"Make the minimum contain the work",minutes:6,goal:"Try a small version of your existing behavior that still produces something useful.",reading:["Keep the goal you chose. Shrink its behavior until it fits today, while keeping the useful part inside. Put the book within reach, then read a sentence. Open the application, then write one useful sentence. The setup is preparation; the sentence is the behavior you want to test.","Habit research describes repeated responses in recurring contexts. It does not show that every tiny start grows, or that a checkmark has the same effect as celebration. Your minimum can stay small. Try it once, record what actually happened, and later ask whether that amount still serves your reason."],practice:["Put one needed item within reach.","Do the actual minimum; stopping afterward is allowed.","Try the minimum: Write one question answer."],reflection:"What useful minimum would serve your chosen reason?",question:"You want to write an application. For three sessions, you opened the file but wrote nothing. You have two minutes and know the required section. Which minimum now tests useful progress?",options:["Reserve a longer session later so a complete answer will feel worth starting.","Open the file earlier and leave it visible, then count the prepared setup for today.","Write one rough sentence for the required section, then stop at the available limit."],correct:2,feedback:"This contains the behavior and leaves an editable piece. It does not guarantee the application's success or require a longer session. Record the sentence separately from opening the file.",takeaway:"Make the start feasible without shrinking the useful behavior out of it.",sources:["M07","M15","M16"],deeper:[{heading:"Small is a design choice, not a guarantee",paragraphs:["Wood and Rünger review habits as learned responses connected with recurring contexts. Repetition can support greater automaticity, while deliberate goals can still matter. Their review does not establish that one sentence is an optimal dose or that every small behavior inevitably expands. This lesson uses a small unit to test access and usefulness in your actual week. [M07]","Use the unit to separate preparation from the behavior you want to practice. A question answered can be checked, a sentence read can convey information, and a sentence written can be edited. Having materials ready is useful setup, but it does not contain those behaviors. If several tiny attempts never serve the reason you chose, review the behavior or goal. You may need a different unit, more support, or a larger feasible window. Do not turn a convenient checkmark into invented progress. [M07]"]},{heading:"The practitioner recipe has limits",paragraphs:["BJ Fogg's Tiny Habits instruction pairs an existing routine with a tiny behavior and recommends revising a pairing that does not work. It is a practitioner technique. The inspected page does not independently establish that its whole recipe outperforms ordinary cue planning or that every action must take less than a minute. [M15]","Fogg also recommends an immediate, self-chosen positive gesture. The proposed claims about faster neural wiring, celebration being necessary, or a checkmark doing the same reinforcing job were not established by controlled evidence in the dossier. [M16] You may notice satisfaction if it occurs, but do not manufacture it. A truthful record says what happened. It is separate from how you felt afterward. You can stop at the useful minimum, retain it tomorrow, and adapt your tools or access method without treating a smaller amount as lesser character."]}],example:{title:"Leila's smaller question",text:"On Tuesday after dinner, Leila has two minutes for the same qualification. She puts her notes within reach, then answers one short practice question. She stops there because family time matters too. Her answer needs correction. She records the answer as work and the notes setup as preparation, without expanding the session."},photo:{id:"1581578731509-8ae9c5a3e7d3",alt:"A pair of shoes waiting by the door"}},{id:"motivation-3",schemaVersion:2,title:"Your cue needs a real opening",minutes:6,goal:"Test your existing minimum at a cue where time, materials, and access are available.",reading:["A cue has three jobs in your experiment: it happens, you notice it, and the behavior is possible then. “After the school run” may be recognizable but useless for desk work if you go straight to your job. Keep your meaningful minimum and choose a setting that actually allows it.","Specific if-then plans can help bridge intention and action. Repeating a behavior in a recurring context can support a habit, but no finish date fits everyone. Today, test the cue rather than trying to become automatic. Record which of the three jobs worked and which needs an adjustment."],practice:["Check: happens, noticed, behavior possible.","Keep the unit; change an unavailable opening.","Try the minimum: Answer one practice question."],reflection:"Which real opening protects time for your chosen goal?",question:"Leila's school-run cue leads straight into her job. Her study materials stay home. A short after-dinner slot at her table is available. Which change addresses the failed condition?",options:["Add a second school-run reminder so she will notice the plan before work begins.","Reduce the school-run target to half a question while keeping the materials at home.","Move the same question to after dinner, with her notes available at the table."],correct:2,feedback:"This changes the unavailable setting while preserving a meaningful unit. Test whether the new cue happens, gets noticed, and permits the behavior before calling the routine reliable.",takeaway:"A usable cue happens, gets noticed, and allows the behavior in that setting.",sources:["M08","M07","M05"],deeper:[{heading:"A cue is a condition to test",paragraphs:["Gollwitzer and Sheeran's 2006 review combined 94 independent tests of plans connecting a situation with a chosen action. Goal attainment improved on average. The historical standardized effect of 0.65 is not a 65 percent improvement, and it does not forecast the effect of your app reminder. These historical averages concern varied goals and settings rather than this exact study cue. [M08]","Wood and Rünger describe context-linked repetition as part of habit learning. A cue alone does not guarantee behavior, and every routine is not necessarily automatic. [M07] ODA's happens, noticed, possible check is a teaching aid. It helps separate a missed reminder from unavailable materials or a competing duty. If the cue did not happen, record no occasion when appropriate. If it happened but you did not act, record that fact without turning it into a verdict about your commitment."]},{heading:"Sixty-six days was a model estimate",paragraphs:["Lally's 2010 study observed chosen daily behaviors for 84 days. Among 39 participants with well-fitting automaticity curves, the median model-estimated time to 95 percent of the plateau was 66 days, with estimates from 18 to 254. The longest estimate extended beyond observation. These were not observed fastest, average, and slowest habit-completion dates. [M05]","Your qualification practice is not a copy of the study's eating, drinking, or activity behaviors. Do not use its estimate as a deadline or proof that practice should become effortless. Keep the same experiment and look at available opportunities, useful behavior, and the setup that supported it. One cue can fit weekdays while a different arrangement is needed during shifts or care. Test a deliberate change and retain the earlier record so your review compares actual conditions."]}],example:{title:"Leila's usable opening",text:"Leila planned her question after the school run, but went straight to work without her notes. On Wednesday she moves the same question to after dinner at the kitchen table. The cue happens and she notices it. She answers before clearing the table. One answer is useful; the earlier unavailable slot stays in her record."},photo:{id:"1499933374294-4584851497cc",alt:"A black alarm clock on a table"}},{id:"motivation-4",schemaVersion:2,title:"Plan for the moment it breaks",minutes:6,goal:"Test one feasible response to an observed obstacle while protecting real needs and responsibilities.",reading:["Keep your goal, minimum, and cue. Use a real attempt to identify the obstacle: a switching habit, missing materials, a competing responsibility, or a health need. Pairing an outcome with an obstacle and a planned response shows modest average benefits in research. It cannot make an unavailable resource appear.","Match the response to the condition. A distracting tab may need a changed setup. Care needs covered time or agreed help. Illness may call for rest. Plan the first useful behavior your response allows, then test it at the next suitable opening. Rehearsal is preparation, not evidence the fallback worked."],practice:["Keep your goal and useful minimum.","Protect real needs; test the feasible response.","Try the minimum: Answer one practice question."],reflection:"What needs protecting while this goal moves forward?",question:"Guitar practice repeatedly overlaps with a child's bedtime. The parent still values practice. An agreed five-minute slot after bedtime is available. Which backup matches the actual obstacle?",options:["Leave the guitar beside the couch so the parent notices the goal during bedtime.","Use the agreed after-bedtime slot for one short practice while keeping childcare covered.","Use the covered five minutes to check tuning and mark the phrase for another practice slot."],correct:1,feedback:"This protects the real responsibility and gives the behavior usable time. The agreement and practice remain separate facts. Record what happened rather than assuming tomorrow will have the same coverage.",takeaway:"A response works only if it fits the obstacle and leaves real needs protected.",sources:["M10","M03"],deeper:[{heading:"Research supports planning, with dose limits",paragraphs:["Wang and colleagues' 2021 review pooled 21 articles and 24 effects of mental contrasting with implementation intentions. Its average goal-attainment effect was small to medium. Results varied across goals and showed publication-bias signals. Combining an outcome, obstacle, and response does not guarantee wishes, demonstrate that thoughts change outside events, or establish every WOOP format as equivalent. [M10]","Duckworth and colleagues tested an academic protocol with 77 fifth graders at one school. Students worked in small groups across three one-hour sessions, with repeated exercises and reminder cards. Some academic outcomes improved, but later between-group differences were nonsignificant. [M03] ODA's short adult exercise changes population, support, and duration. It is an adaptation for choosing a testable response, not the full intervention. Rehearse if useful, then distinguish preparation from the actual opportunity when the obstacle appears and the response is needed."]},{heading:"Match the barrier without blaming the person",paragraphs:["The planning procedure examines a desired outcome, an obstacle, and a chosen counteraction. It does not establish that every obstacle is an inner attitude. Care, money, disability access, shift work, illness, and another person's harmful conduct can constrain what is feasible. A practical response may be agreed support, a changed schedule, an accommodation, rest, or a boundary. The adult adaptation should name those constraints honestly. [M03, M10]","Use your actual record to choose one condition to change. If the cue happened and materials were unavailable, a bigger commitment misses the problem. If care needed you, leave that responsibility covered. If a distracting habit appeared, test a response that still allows urgent contact. An obstacle that never occurred leaves its fallback untested. Record the useful unit separately from arranging help or moving materials. Later, consolidate the setup that worked rather than collecting more rituals or demanding a stronger feeling of motivation."]}],example:{title:"Leila's covered time",text:"Thursday's study cue arrives during Leila's child's bedtime. Her partner agrees to cover five minutes after bedtime, so she moves the same question there. She answers at the table while care remains covered. She records the arrangement and the answer. The question needs work, and tomorrow's coverage is not assumed."},photo:{id:"1575478004055-999ebe26dab7",alt:"A woman walking in the rain with an umbrella"}},{id:"motivation-5",schemaVersion:2,title:"A missed day needs a workable return",minutes:6,goal:"Review the same experiment, retain one useful condition, and schedule a feasible next attempt.",reading:["Review your chosen reason, useful minimum, cue, and actual records. Progress monitoring can support goal attainment on average. It cannot turn an untried plan into behavior or make blank history mean failure. Keep preparation, actual work, and pause distinct when you decide what helped.","One missed opportunity did not materially disrupt habit formation in one study. That does not establish that long interruptions are harmless. Use your own conditions: repeat a setup that fit, change an unavailable cue, or reconsider the goal. Schedule the next useful unit. No doubling up, new ritual, or earned permission to rest is required."],practice:["Keep one condition that helped.","Change one barrier; schedule the same useful unit.","Try the minimum: Answer one practice question."],reflection:"Does this goal still deserve a place next week?",question:"Kofi missed four walks after his evening shift changed. Walking still matters to him. A short safe lunchtime route is available. Which return addresses the recorded barrier?",options:["Choose the available lunch cue, take the short safe walk, and record the return.","Keep the old dinner cue and reduce the walk to one loop around the block.","Prepare favorite audio for the old evening slot so the return feels more inviting."],correct:0,feedback:"This keeps the chosen goal while changing the condition that failed. The actual walk, not the planned return, is the behavior to record. Compare whether the lunch cue continues to fit.",takeaway:"Keep the reason and useful behavior; change the condition your record says has failed.",sources:["M13","M05","M20"],deeper:[{heading:"Consolidate evidence without inventing a streak",paragraphs:["Harkin and colleagues' review of 138 randomized studies found an average goal-attainment benefit from progress monitoring. Goals and methods varied. Recording and reporting moderators were associated with larger effects, but those results do not establish that public sharing or a weekly cadence is necessary. ODA's next-day return and brief weekly review are adaptations, not validated doses. [M13]","Lally's observational study found that one missed opportunity did not materially disrupt its modeled habit-formation process. That narrow result does not prove long interruptions leave a habit unchanged. [M05] Use your own attempts to choose what comes next. Keep a useful unit, change a cue or response that did not fit, and leave historical misses visible. A future plan is still planned. If the goal no longer deserves its time cost, reshape, defer, or stop it deliberately. Learning completion is separate from doing and reviewing the behavior."]},{heading:"Enjoyment is optional; support can come now",paragraphs:["Your final lesson consolidates the experiment rather than adding a new motivational ritual. Monitoring research supports comparing actual progress with a chosen goal on average, but it does not identify which technique is best for every person. Your record can show whether a cue was unavailable, whether materials were missing, or whether the goal's time cost stopped fitting. Use that observed condition to choose one next change. Do not count preparation or a promise as the behavior. [M13]","Do not make basic rest or all enjoyable activity conditional on productivity. If you later choose compatible free audio, preserve traffic and safety awareness. Persistent loss of interest, distress, exhaustion, or trouble completing usual activities may need professional assessment. NIMH guidance supports seeking help and immediate assistance for urgent danger; it does not diagnose the cause from low motivation or require waiting before asking for help. [M20] You can keep a small useful practice, request support, or pause while that concern is addressed."]}],example:{title:"Leila's next opening",text:"On Sunday, Leila reviews the same qualification plan. Dinner slots worked twice; a changed shift blocked two others. She still chooses the goal, so she moves one question to Monday's available lunch break with accessible notes. She writes down the next opening. The missed sessions stay missed, and Monday's action remains a plan."},photo:{id:"1577950248429-7530424d9682",alt:"A monthly calendar pinned to a board"}}].map((t,a)=>{const e=i[a];return{...t,schemaVersion:2,title:e.title,goal:e.goal,reading:e.idea.paragraphs,deeper:e.goDeeper.sections,example:e.idea.example,takeaway:e.carry.takeaway,v2:e}})};export{n as COURSE,i as LESSONS_V2,o as SOURCES};
